WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.000
How much do we really know about Jupiter?

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.959
The majestic gas giant of our solar system

00:00:09.959 --> 00:00:14.459
is shrouded in cloud cover and storms the last centuries.

00:00:14.459 --> 00:00:17.670
So the planet remains a bit of a mystery.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.300
That's about to change.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.750
After nearly five years in space,

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:26.000
NASA's Juno probe will finally reach
Jupiter on July 4th.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.400
Juno's arrival marks the start of an unprecedented peek beneath the planet's clouds.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.900
The mechanical probe launched back in 2011.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:41.000
With a little help from Earth's gravity,
Juno managed to slingshot itself toward Jupiter.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
The trip covered nearly 2.8 billion kilometers

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.499
Once Juno reaches, Jupiter it will
need to reorient itself and slow down

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.800
to hitch a ride in orbit with the giant
world.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.000
Juno will spend 20 months whizzing
around the planet 37 times.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.200
The probe will swoop by on an elongated track,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.300
buzzing Jupiter's poles and skimming its clouds

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.520
at a quarter of a million kilometers per hour.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:16.500
Here's what that will look like from Juno's perspective.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:21.750
This unique view will give Juno the opportunity to answer some big questions about Jupiter...

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.500
like how much water does the gas giant
have?

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:28.000
What do Jupiter's auroras look like up close?

00:01:28.229 --> 00:01:32.000
And what can they tell us about the gas
giant's magnetic field?

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.200
Does Jupiter have a solid core or is the planet's center gooey?

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:41.500
Juno's observations could reveal how the giant formed over four billion years ago.

00:01:41.789 --> 00:01:46.200
Understanding Jupiter's beginnings also
provides a glimpse of our early solar system

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.000
and how planets form in general.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.000
Juno's arrival will be a soft opening for a much bigger show.

